    "description": "Iran's medical history connects medieval Arabic and Persian scholarship with later changes in teaching, terminology, and professional institutions. Medical compendia, practical manuals, and the nineteenth-century Dar al-Fonun illustrate different ways that knowledge was organized and transmitted.",
    "tags": ["Medical history", "Avicenna", "Persian manuscripts", "Dar al-Fonun", "Medical education"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-26T15:32:53Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-26T15:32:53Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "History of Medicine in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover Avicenna's Canon, early Persian medical writing, and the transformation of medical education through Dar al-Fonun."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "texts-and-teaching",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A history of texts, languages, and teaching",
        "content": "Iran's medical history cannot be contained within today's national borders or a single language. Bukhara, now in Uzbekistan, figures prominently in accounts of early Persian medical writing. Arabic, meanwhile, enabled scholars from different regions to address a wider learned readership. The language of a medical book therefore does not by itself establish its author's cultural identity or the geography of its influence. [s1]\n\nDifferent books served different purposes. A systematic compendium could arrange the principles of medicine, while a practical manual could help students and physicians consult treatments. Reading these works as distinct genres makes their contributions clearer than treating every celebrated author as the originator of the same medical tradition. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "persian-medical-manual",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A practical medical voice in Persian",
        "content": "Abu Bakr Rabi ibn Ahmad Akhawayni Bukhari composed the *Hedayat al-Motaallemin fi Tebb*, a guide for medical learners, around 983–984 CE. A historical study identifies it as the oldest known medical treatise in Persian. Its significance lies partly in making medical explanation available through Persian prose and terminology at a time when Arabic dominated major scholarly works. [s1]\n\nThe same study emphasizes Akhawayni's practical approach: he reports recommendations inherited from earlier physicians and adds experience of treatments, including failures. Such passages provide evidence of an author evaluating practice, rather than merely copying an authoritative system. They do not establish that every treatment was effective by present standards, nor that a surviving manual represents the care available to the whole population. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "canon-and-medical-order",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Avicenna and the organization of medicine",
        "content": "Ibn Sina, commonly known in English as Avicenna, organized the *Canon of Medicine* into five books covering general principles, individual medicines, diseases of particular organs, conditions affecting the body more generally, and compound remedies. Its architecture made a large body of knowledge available in an ordered form. [s2]\n\nThe *Canon* was grounded in Galenic medicine, named after the ancient physician Galen. Its explanation of the body used concepts such as elements, humors, and temperaments. Encyclopaedia Iranica stresses that sophisticated medical compendia already existed before Avicenna: his achievement should not erase predecessors such as Razi and Ali ibn Abbas Majusi. [s2]\n\nAvicenna also tried to distinguish medicine's proper subject from natural philosophy, even though the fields overlapped. This reveals an intellectual problem as well as a clinical one: who could establish the principles on which explanations of health and disease depended? Historical importance and present clinical validity are separate questions; these theories are presented here as historical knowledge, not treatment guidance. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "dar-al-fonun",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Dar al-Fonun and a changing profession",
        "content": "A major nineteenth-century change came through Dar al-Fonun, the Tehran polytechnic founded in 1851 under the reforming minister Amir Kabir. Medicine, surgery, and pharmacology formed part of a broader program that also included technical and military subjects. The Austrian physician Jakob Eduard Polak became an important instructor. [s3]\n\nTeaching required more than importing books. Instructors faced language barriers and the need to establish Persian equivalents for unfamiliar medical terminology. Polak learned Persian and contributed teaching materials; Iranian graduates who studied abroad subsequently became instructors themselves. This was a process of translation and institutional development, rather than a single moment when an entire medical system changed. [s3]\n\nTraditional and European medicine also coexisted within the school. Iranica records an 1860 decision to teach both, illustrating negotiation over public confidence and professional authority. A later historical study places the medical department's independence in 1918 and the formal establishment of the University of Tehran's medical school in 1934. Together, these developments mark a shift toward specialized educational institutions; they do not by themselves measure access to treatment or improvements in population health. [s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
